1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
The plan was daring.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:09,000
To unite a nation with 1,700 miles of track across untamed wilderness,

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
over snow-capped peaks, and through solid rock.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Two rival workforces in a great race to link the American continent.

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Each facing deadly obstacles and titanic challenges of engineering.

6
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,400
Now, the Transcontinental Railroad on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:49,171 --> 00:00:54,700
Around the globe, high-speed trains scorch the landscape at up to 200 miles per hour.

8
00:00:54,701 --> 00:00:58,240
whisking passengers to destinations so fast

9
00:00:58,241 --> 00:01:00,680
they're giving the airlines a run for their money.

10
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:03,780
In a lightning-paced world

11
00:01:03,781 --> 00:01:05,680
where everyone's watching the clock,

12
00:01:06,180 --> 00:01:07,160
velocity is a virtue.

13
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:11,540
In mid-19th century America,

14
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:15,700
the fastest trains chugged along at just 25 miles per hour

15
00:01:15,701 --> 00:01:18,640
and no rail line ventured further west

16
00:01:18,641 --> 00:01:21,980
than the town of St. Joseph on the Kansas-Missouri border.

17
00:01:21,981 --> 00:01:30,480
But despite the railroad's limitations, men of vision and men of greed viewed it as the

18
00:01:30,481 --> 00:01:35,420
wonder that could unify and transform the far-flung nation.

19
00:01:35,421 --> 00:01:40,680
They envisioned an American continent linked from coast to coast by a road of iron, an

20
00:01:40,681 --> 00:01:45,100
avenue to economic glory and boundless opportunity.

21
00:01:45,101 --> 00:01:49,960
Engineering such a dream, however, seemed impossible.

22
00:01:49,961 --> 00:01:55,740
the established east and the new territories of the west sprawled nearly 2,000 miles of

23
00:01:55,741 --> 00:01:58,522
virgin wilderness and hostile terrain.

24
00:01:58,523 --> 00:02:03,060
If you try to picture what North America was like in the 1840s

25
00:02:03,061 --> 00:02:08,420
and 50s when the whole notion of the railroad began, you're basically talking about a vast

26
00:02:08,421 --> 00:02:13,476
emptiness beyond first the Mississippi and then the Missouri River.

27
00:02:13,477 --> 00:02:15,740
And beyond that, empty plains,

28
00:02:15,741 --> 00:02:22,660
a few scattered villages, several mountain ranges such as the Rockies and the Wasatch and the Sierra Nevadas,

29
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:29,660
and basically that was the whole area that had to be crossed as the notion of the Transcontinental Railroad began.

30
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,620
And it was clearly an impossibility back then.

31
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:38,760
Since the 1840s, pioneers had defied the vast expanse separating East and West,

32
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:42,260
seeking land or gold in California and Oregon.

33
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:46,840
Those lucky enough to survive the trek endured five months of misery.

34
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:50,920
Well, the hardship of emigrating was the time,

35
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:55,640
the fact that you might start out with all your worldly goods in a Conestoga wagon

36
00:02:55,641 --> 00:02:58,900
and discard things along the way in order to be able to keep moving.

37
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:01,960
There might be Indian attacks.

38
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:07,120
There might be illnesses that your family members would contract and wouldn't survive.

39
00:03:07,121 --> 00:03:14,120
The only alternative for those heading west was a long ocean voyage that carried its own

40
00:03:14,121 --> 00:03:15,120
hazards.

41
00:03:15,121 --> 00:03:19,980
You could come on a ship all the way around South America.

42
00:03:19,981 --> 00:03:25,240
You could take that same ship and instead go over the isthmus of Panama, where you had

43
00:03:25,241 --> 00:03:28,720
a great chance of catching malaria.

44
00:03:28,721 --> 00:03:33,440
No one knew the difficulties and dangers of sea travel better than an idealistic merchant

45
00:03:33,441 --> 00:03:36,440
named Asa Whitney.

46
00:03:36,441 --> 00:03:44,600
In 1844, Whitney became one of the first Americans to trade in China.

47
00:03:44,601 --> 00:03:48,740
He believed a transcontinental railroad would not only help America tap China's commercial

48
00:03:48,741 --> 00:03:53,740
wealth, but also foster American unity.

49
00:03:53,741 --> 00:03:58,320
On his way back from the Orient, he realized that if there was a railroad that went across

50
00:03:58,321 --> 00:04:03,200
the United States, it would be a much shorter trip than having to go all the way down around

51
00:04:03,201 --> 00:04:05,560
South America before they even got into the Pacific.

52
00:04:05,561 --> 00:04:07,880
who needed a railroad across the continent.

53
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:11,100
The big issue was how are you going to finance this thing.

54
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:17,360
In January 1845, Whitney went before the United States Congress

55
00:04:17,361 --> 00:04:18,900
to present his grandiose scheme,

56
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:22,180
as well as an inventive strategy for financing it.

57
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:24,620
His idea was a very good one.

58
00:04:24,621 --> 00:04:28,320
The federal government would give a certain amount of land to the railroad,

59
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:31,000
which the railroad could use for the right-of-way,

60
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:32,640
and then sell off that land.

61
00:04:32,641 --> 00:04:36,080
The money from that sale would allow them to build the next segment.

62
00:04:38,080 --> 00:04:42,400
Although Whitney's proposal was feasible, short-sighted congressmen rejected it.

63
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:47,400
Undaunted, he promoted his dream in the press for the next six years.

64
00:04:48,620 --> 00:04:53,260
Asa Whitney put the whole notion of the Transcontinental Railroad onto the national agenda,

65
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:59,280
single-handedly, using his own money, persuading people just by the force of his personality.

66
00:04:59,281 --> 00:05:05,060
In 1851, however, Congress once again rejected his proposal.

67
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:08,080
The disheartened Whitney retreated into obscurity.

68
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:12,460
But his vision gained newfound momentum in the 1850s,

69
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:16,620
as California's gold rush continued to turn the nation's attention west.

70
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,900
Tens of thousands of emigrants fighting their way across this terrible and empty terrain.

71
00:05:24,901 --> 00:05:33,380
And at the same point, these untold riches coming up out of the earth, out of the mountains, out of the streams of California and Nevada.

72
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:37,880
And it really made the whole issue so much more urgent.

73
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:40,360
Made it an inevitability.

74
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:50,680
The inevitability of a coast-to-coast railroad captured the imagination of American engineers, including an ambitious New Yorker, Theodore Judah.

75
00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:58,340
In 1854, he ventured west to build California's first set of tracks in the Sacramento Valley.

76
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:03,660
It was then that he began to have these grandiose dreams,

77
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:08,920
these lunatic dreams about somehow being connected with the Transcontinental Railroad.

78
00:06:09,620 --> 00:06:12,400
He knew it could be done, and he devoted every waking hour

79
00:06:12,401 --> 00:06:16,180
and every increment of his energy to convincing others that it was possible.

80
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:19,620
He was known as Crazy Judah and called that to his face.

81
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:24,180
No one could curb Judah's passion for his transcontinental dream.

82
00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:27,980
But his work in California gave him firsthand knowledge

83
00:06:27,981 --> 00:06:30,280
of the daunting natural barrier that could crush it.

84
00:06:31,420 --> 00:06:35,060
The Sierra Nevada, which spans 400 miles

85
00:06:35,061 --> 00:06:38,280
and features peaks more than 14,000 feet high.

86
00:06:39,560 --> 00:06:41,680
Judah's challenge was to find a railroad

87
00:06:41,681 --> 00:06:42,920
through the mountainous terrain.

88
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:45,900
Several expeditions yielded nothing.

89
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:48,220
Then came a ray of hope.

90
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:52,100
When Judah got back to Sacramento, he had a letter from Doc Strong,

91
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:55,460
who was a pharmacist up in Dutch Flat, a little town up in the mountains.

92
00:06:56,200 --> 00:07:00,780
Strong was interested in rebuilding the old road that ran through Dutch Flat

93
00:07:00,781 --> 00:07:03,780
on over Donner Summit to the Nevada desert beyond.

94
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:08,540
And Doc Strong told him about this single continuous divide

95
00:07:08,541 --> 00:07:10,380
up the North Fork of the American River.

96
00:07:10,381 --> 00:07:13,340
And this was the answer to his prayers.

97
00:07:13,341 --> 00:07:21,080
In October 1860, Strong led Judah on a difficult two-day trek up the Sierra to Donner Summit,

98
00:07:21,640 --> 00:07:26,120
where the ill-fated party of settlers had become snowbound just 14 years earlier.

99
00:07:27,620 --> 00:07:33,600
So there Judah and Doc Strong stood on the summit of Donner Pass with the ruts of the

100
00:07:33,601 --> 00:07:39,420
doomed Donner party just at their feet, looking over that great gulf over the lake and off toward

101
00:07:39,421 --> 00:07:43,980
the east, and Judah realized that this was the magic route.

102
00:07:44,860 --> 00:07:49,940
The magic route followed an immigrant trail virtually abandoned after the Donner tragedy,

103
00:07:50,840 --> 00:07:55,179
a ridgeline that formed a natural ramp up the mountains.

104
00:07:55,180 --> 00:07:58,200
Elated by his discovery, Judah returned

105
00:07:58,201 --> 00:08:03,700
with Strong to draft articles of incorporation for what they called the Central Pacific Railroad

106
00:08:03,701 --> 00:08:04,401
company.

107
00:08:04,402 --> 00:08:10,660
One prodigious obstacle still remained, finding investors to help finance it. Five

108
00:08:10,661 --> 00:08:14,089
Sacramento businessmen answered his challenge.

109
00:08:14,090 --> 00:08:17,220
Hardware wholesaler Collis Huntington, his

110
00:08:17,221 --> 00:08:24,220
business partner Mark Hopkins, dry goods dealer Charles Crocker, Crocker's brother attorney E.B.

111
00:08:24,221 --> 00:08:28,182
Crocker, and wholesale grocer Leland Stanford.

112
00:08:28,183 --> 00:08:31,800
The five Republicans called themselves the

113
00:08:31,801 --> 00:08:32,501
associates.

114
00:08:32,502 --> 00:08:37,720
All of the associates had come over to California as a result of the gold rush, and they

115
00:08:37,721 --> 00:08:42,820
had made their money not from digging gold, but from selling equipment and other things to the

116
00:08:42,821 --> 00:08:44,369
gold miners themselves.

117
00:08:44,370 --> 00:08:49,220
So they got to be very well off that way, and it was a natural thing for

118
00:08:49,221 --> 00:08:55,041
them to want to invest in something that was going to help build up California.

119
00:08:55,042 --> 00:08:56,220
In October 1861,

120
00:08:56,221 --> 00:08:58,960
Judas set out to pitch his plan in the nation's capital.

121
00:09:00,220 --> 00:09:03,300
As the nation convulsed in the first year of the Civil War,

122
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,360
many congressmen had no interest in supporting such a risky and costly endeavor.

123
00:09:10,380 --> 00:09:14,840
But Judas' plan found a steadfast champion in President Abraham Lincoln.

124
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:18,820
For Abraham Lincoln, the creation of the Pacific Railroad

125
00:09:18,821 --> 00:09:24,280
was not so much a commercial act or an act of technology as it was a political act.

126
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:27,200
We had to connect the East with the West

127
00:09:27,201 --> 00:09:29,820
in the same way as Lincoln was trying to preserve

128
00:09:29,821 --> 00:09:32,600
the North and the South as one country.

129
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:36,260
The whole notion of the transcontinental railroad

130
00:09:36,261 --> 00:09:38,180
becomes imperative for the war effort.

131
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:42,120
All those millions of dollars coming out of California and Nevada

132
00:09:42,121 --> 00:09:47,480
and somehow the West Coast has got to be kept to the Union.

133
00:09:47,900 --> 00:09:51,320
And of course, so then the railroad becomes a war measure

134
00:09:51,321 --> 00:09:54,160
and it's got to be pushed through as quickly as possible.

135
00:09:54,280 --> 00:10:01,660
On July 1, 1862, Lincoln signed a bill that guaranteed the Central Pacific the right to

136
00:10:01,661 --> 00:10:08,180
build east from Sacramento, and a second company, the Union Pacific, the right to build west

137
00:10:08,181 --> 00:10:09,743
from the Missouri River.

138
00:10:09,744 --> 00:10:14,040
The bill didn't specify where the two companies would meet, but it

139
00:10:14,041 --> 00:10:17,040
did provide an incentive for them to move quickly.

140
00:10:17,041 --> 00:10:19,500
For each mile of track laid, the builders

141
00:10:19,501 --> 00:10:23,420
would be granted 640 acres of adjoining land.

142
00:10:23,421 --> 00:10:26,360
The legislation pitted the two companies against each other

143
00:10:26,361 --> 00:10:31,000
in a titanic race to acquire as much land as possible.

144
00:10:31,001 --> 00:10:33,580
The government kept alternate sections

145
00:10:33,581 --> 00:10:36,060
throughout all of this also so that the government

146
00:10:36,061 --> 00:10:40,180
could profit because the railroad increased the land values.

147
00:10:40,181 --> 00:10:43,680
In addition to the land grants, the government also

148
00:10:43,681 --> 00:10:47,380
provided bonds based on the certification

149
00:10:47,381 --> 00:10:48,860
of completion of the track.

150
00:10:49,240 --> 00:10:51,120
But these bonds were loans.

151
00:10:51,360 --> 00:10:52,420
These were not gifts.

152
00:10:53,960 --> 00:10:55,800
Even so, they were generous.

153
00:10:56,840 --> 00:11:00,060
The railroad companies would be lent $16,000 per mile

154
00:11:00,061 --> 00:11:01,620
for track laid in flatland,

155
00:11:02,480 --> 00:11:04,960
$32,000 per mile in foothills,

156
00:11:05,460 --> 00:11:08,400
and $48,000 per mile in the mountains.

157
00:11:10,740 --> 00:11:13,340
But the legislation wasn't without strings.

158
00:11:14,260 --> 00:11:17,000
The most sobering provision called for each company

159
00:11:17,001 --> 00:11:23,240
to forfeit every acre and penny granted and all other company assets if the railroad wasn't

160
00:11:23,241 --> 00:11:29,640
finished within 12 years. Judah had every faith that his vision could be fulfilled,

161
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:36,029
but his faith in the Sacramento investors, the associates, was sinking fast.

162
00:11:36,030 --> 00:11:37,360
Judah feared they

163
00:11:37,361 --> 00:11:41,540
were far more concerned with lining their own pockets than building a quality railroad.

164
00:11:42,400 --> 00:11:46,880
Theodore Judah went east, hoping to get some other investors,

165
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:51,280
and he had an agreement to buy out the associates of their pieces of the Central Pacific,

166
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:52,960
and they would have just walked away from it.

167
00:11:54,100 --> 00:11:59,460
But during his long voyage to America's east coast, Judah contracted yellow fever.

168
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:06,940
And on November 2, 1863, five days after reaching New York, he died.

169
00:12:11,540 --> 00:12:17,180
Just a week earlier in California, the first rails of the Central Pacific had been laid.

170
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:23,120
The project that had become his obsession was now controlled by the investors he mistrusted.

171
00:12:30,780 --> 00:12:36,900
And the gun had sounded for the great race to see who could hurl the most track across the country.

172
00:12:36,901 --> 00:12:45,780
In 1830, there were 23 miles of working railroad track in the United States.

173
00:12:46,480 --> 00:12:49,360
By 1850, more than 9,000 miles.

174
00:12:49,800 --> 00:12:52,900
And by 1860, more than 30,000 miles.

175
00:12:56,900 --> 00:13:03,680
8,000 locomotives, 107,000 freight cars, 32,000 miles of track.

176
00:13:03,681 --> 00:13:08,680
the vital statistics of North America's largest railroad, the Union Pacific.

177
00:13:10,660 --> 00:13:13,120
After carrying passengers for over a century,

178
00:13:13,800 --> 00:13:17,200
it's forged its reputation over the past three decades as a freight carrier.

179
00:13:18,740 --> 00:13:21,380
It boasts an annual revenue of $12 billion.

180
00:13:24,200 --> 00:13:29,940
No one would have savored that figure more than a self-serving tycoon named Thomas Clark Durant,

181
00:13:30,340 --> 00:13:32,220
the first vice president of the company.

182
00:13:33,120 --> 00:13:35,880
Under his leadership and lack of scruples,

183
00:13:36,600 --> 00:13:39,540
the Union Pacific broke ground on the Transcontinental Railroad

184
00:13:39,541 --> 00:13:42,340
near Omaha, Nebraska, in 1863.

185
00:13:43,720 --> 00:13:46,540
Completing the grand undertaking was less important to Durant

186
00:13:46,541 --> 00:13:49,300
than pocketing as much cash as possible along the way.

187
00:13:50,340 --> 00:13:52,640
He was the first genius for manipulation,

188
00:13:52,960 --> 00:13:55,680
according to one of the people who worked for him.

189
00:13:55,760 --> 00:13:56,720
He was a schemer.

190
00:13:57,340 --> 00:14:01,540
He was trained as an ophthalmologist in upstate New York.

191
00:14:01,541 --> 00:14:05,480
but he really found that his greater talent was making money.

192
00:14:06,240 --> 00:14:10,600
Durant created a clandestine finance company called Credit Mobilier

193
00:14:10,601 --> 00:14:13,460
to pay the Union Pacific's construction costs.

194
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:17,740
By exaggerating his production costs, he stood to make an illicit fortune.

195
00:14:18,880 --> 00:14:21,420
More interested in profits than progress,

196
00:14:22,080 --> 00:14:25,720
he witnessed his track layers advance just 40 miles in two years

197
00:14:25,721 --> 00:14:27,780
over the route's most gentle terrain.

198
00:14:28,780 --> 00:14:32,660
Across the continent, the Central Pacific had progressed even less,

199
00:14:33,260 --> 00:14:35,620
but its construction teams had a much better excuse.

200
00:14:36,700 --> 00:14:39,960
The Central Pacific, right from the beginning,

201
00:14:40,200 --> 00:14:44,180
faced the biggest hurdle that they would experience in their entire construction,

202
00:14:44,800 --> 00:14:47,260
building across the Sierra Nevada Mountains,

203
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:51,080
a route that was thought to be impossible by many people.

204
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:54,840
The Central Pacific's co-owner and construction chief,

205
00:14:54,841 --> 00:14:57,680
Charlie Crocker, took on the formidable task.

206
00:14:57,780 --> 00:15:03,740
He was not by training or nature prepared to build a railroad across the mountains,

207
00:15:03,741 --> 00:15:07,720
but he possessed a rare common sense, a gift of leading men.

208
00:15:08,260 --> 00:15:11,480
And he knew enough engineering to be able to size up a situation,

209
00:15:11,940 --> 00:15:14,360
know what materials he needed, and actually make it happen.

210
00:15:15,920 --> 00:15:18,700
Crocker's immediate problem wasn't the mountains themselves,

211
00:15:19,280 --> 00:15:20,840
but the manpower to move them.

212
00:15:22,620 --> 00:15:24,480
California's labor force was scant,

213
00:15:24,880 --> 00:15:27,640
and most men near the Sierra were busy in the gold fields,

214
00:15:27,780 --> 00:15:29,740
or at the Comstock Mines in Nevada.

215
00:15:30,660 --> 00:15:33,960
And the associates advertised for 5,000 workers

216
00:15:33,961 --> 00:15:36,940
to really get out and begin addressing those mountains.

217
00:15:37,600 --> 00:15:38,920
And 200 showed up.

218
00:15:39,380 --> 00:15:42,180
And they were just beside themselves, what are we going to do?

219
00:15:42,300 --> 00:15:45,360
And Charlie Crocker had the idea, why don't we try some of those Chinese?

220
00:15:45,580 --> 00:15:49,240
They were doing all the menial labor around Sacramento and so on.

221
00:15:50,380 --> 00:15:52,240
Everybody scoffed at Charlie Crocker

222
00:15:52,241 --> 00:15:54,940
because he hired a few of the Chinese

223
00:15:54,941 --> 00:15:58,120
to test them out, see how good they were.

224
00:15:58,760 --> 00:16:01,720
And they said, oh, they're too short, they're too small in stature,

225
00:16:01,840 --> 00:16:03,400
they can't do that kind of work.

226
00:16:04,360 --> 00:16:06,700
And they were soon proved wrong.

227
00:16:08,500 --> 00:16:13,000
But Crocker's Chinese laborers, comprising 80% of his workforce,

228
00:16:13,001 --> 00:16:15,460
still needed tons of supplies and machinery.

229
00:16:16,400 --> 00:16:20,780
And California had no industrial means to produce locomotives or rail equipment.

230
00:16:20,781 --> 00:16:27,780
Everything had to be imported around Cape Horn, all the iron, all the spikes, all the

231
00:16:27,781 --> 00:16:30,580
engines, all the rolling stock.

232
00:16:30,581 --> 00:16:35,040
And they really wouldn't get paid by the United States government until they got a continuous

233
00:16:35,041 --> 00:16:36,040
line going.

234
00:16:36,041 --> 00:16:40,220
At times there were dozens of ships on the way to California.

235
00:16:40,221 --> 00:16:45,160
On an average it took about five months for a ship to get from New York to San Francisco.

236
00:16:45,161 --> 00:16:49,540
They had to go all the way down through the Atlantic around the south end of South America

237
00:16:49,541 --> 00:16:51,780
and then worked their way up the Pacific Ocean

238
00:16:51,781 --> 00:16:53,640
until they finally got to San Francisco.

239
00:16:55,240 --> 00:16:58,840
From San Francisco, supplies were ferried upriver to Sacramento.

240
00:16:59,940 --> 00:17:02,540
From there, workers hauled them up into the Sierra

241
00:17:02,541 --> 00:17:04,960
in horse or oxen-drawn wagons.

242
00:17:06,420 --> 00:17:09,620
The wagons also came in handy during the first crucial step

243
00:17:09,621 --> 00:17:11,140
in the railroad building process,

244
00:17:12,360 --> 00:17:13,520
greeting the steep terrain.

245
00:17:14,780 --> 00:17:16,160
Laborers sculpted the landscape

246
00:17:16,161 --> 00:17:19,160
using an efficient method called cut and fill.

247
00:17:19,540 --> 00:17:27,780
They are cutting out areas through the rock, but they are also filling in over canyons.

248
00:17:28,040 --> 00:17:35,180
And so they want to reuse the dirt and rock from the cuts in the fill to be as efficient as possible,

249
00:17:35,181 --> 00:17:38,120
so they don't have to bring in extra material for the fills,

250
00:17:38,360 --> 00:17:42,500
or so they don't have to haul away and get rid of extra material from the cuts.

251
00:17:43,060 --> 00:17:45,700
The grading crews followed behind the surveyors.

252
00:17:46,500 --> 00:17:48,760
The surveyor's job was to carefully plot out a route,

253
00:17:49,100 --> 00:17:50,940
no steeper than a 2% grade,

254
00:17:51,460 --> 00:17:53,320
to accommodate the era's locomotives.

255
00:17:54,640 --> 00:17:56,000
Before there was a railroad here,

256
00:17:56,080 --> 00:17:57,300
it just would have been a hillside.

257
00:17:57,880 --> 00:17:59,400
The surveyors would have come along,

258
00:17:59,480 --> 00:18:01,060
driving stakes to show the workers

259
00:18:01,061 --> 00:18:02,680
where the railroad was supposed to be built.

260
00:18:03,740 --> 00:18:06,340
Then maybe days, maybe weeks later,

261
00:18:06,380 --> 00:18:07,960
the construction men would come along

262
00:18:07,961 --> 00:18:10,740
and they would start scraping out the ground.

263
00:18:10,741 --> 00:18:12,320
They would be cutting back into the hill

264
00:18:12,321 --> 00:18:13,740
and bringing fill out this way

265
00:18:13,741 --> 00:18:18,200
to create a nice level bed, maybe 14, maybe 16 feet wide,

266
00:18:18,320 --> 00:18:19,860
that they could build the railroad on.

267
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:24,960
Next, workers laid out the wooden cross ties that formed a base for the rails.

268
00:18:26,340 --> 00:18:29,080
Then, finally, as the track layers come along,

269
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:33,620
they've got a train with supplies, primarily the rail and the spikes.

270
00:18:34,220 --> 00:18:37,240
Out in front of that train, there'd be a flat car with the rail on it.

271
00:18:37,241 --> 00:18:42,220
Men would drag the rail 24-foot, 30-foot lengths of rail off the end of the car.

272
00:18:42,221 --> 00:18:46,480
An efficient crew could lay a rail every 30 seconds.

273
00:18:47,680 --> 00:18:53,600
After the first was laid, a worker with a track gauge took a measurement to determine where the second rail should be placed.

274
00:18:55,120 --> 00:19:00,420
Positioning them the proper distance apart was critical to preventing locomotives in their cars from derailing.

275
00:19:03,220 --> 00:19:07,660
After workers had accurately placed both rails, the spike drivers took over.

276
00:19:08,520 --> 00:19:10,680
They pounded in six-inch iron spikes

277
00:19:10,681 --> 00:19:13,400
that secured the rails to the wooden cross ties.

278
00:19:14,780 --> 00:19:19,340
Workers then joined the rails using fish plates or iron splice bars.

279
00:19:20,280 --> 00:19:22,140
There are two of them per joint,

280
00:19:22,680 --> 00:19:26,480
one on the inside of the rail and one on the outside of the rail,

281
00:19:26,481 --> 00:19:28,820
and the bolts would go through these bolt holes

282
00:19:28,821 --> 00:19:33,000
and secure the ends of the rails together in one continuous unit.

283
00:19:34,560 --> 00:19:37,260
Laying track could be as dangerous as it was demanding.

284
00:19:37,660 --> 00:19:39,900
but laborers took it all in stride.

285
00:19:40,700 --> 00:19:42,700
It was just part of the occupation.

286
00:19:43,440 --> 00:19:46,740
For example, if a person was driving a spike with a spike ball

287
00:19:46,741 --> 00:19:49,400
and missed the spike head and hit his ankle,

288
00:19:49,500 --> 00:19:52,760
you would have a fracture or a seriously injured ankle.

289
00:19:54,040 --> 00:19:56,340
Dangers much more deadly faced the workers

290
00:19:56,341 --> 00:19:58,520
who placed and detonated explosive charges

291
00:19:58,521 --> 00:20:00,780
to blast through the Sierra's rock.

292
00:20:01,980 --> 00:20:04,300
The best precaution was precision.

293
00:20:05,080 --> 00:20:06,420
This is a double jack.

294
00:20:06,421 --> 00:20:08,420
Two men would handle this.

295
00:20:08,421 --> 00:20:12,420
One guy would hold the bar and turn it as the other man hit it,

296
00:20:12,421 --> 00:20:18,420
and hit it, and hit it, and eventually you end up with a hole like this.

297
00:20:18,421 --> 00:20:25,420
You pour your black powder down there, put a wick in it, skedaddle, and it blows it out.

298
00:20:26,420 --> 00:20:30,420
The Central Pacific's powder teams faced one of their most dangerous assignments here,

299
00:20:30,421 --> 00:20:36,420
blasting a wedge 800 feet long to create what became known as Bloomer Cut.

300
00:20:36,421 --> 00:20:44,420
45 men dug this out between February of 1864 and April of 1865.

301
00:20:44,421 --> 00:20:48,420
The reason only 45 men were working is because of the black powder they were using.

302
00:20:48,421 --> 00:20:51,420
It's only 800, it's 800 feet long, only 800 feet.

303
00:20:51,421 --> 00:20:56,420
If it's 800 feet long, if you had more than 40 guys in here, you'd have a guy over so many feet.

304
00:20:56,421 --> 00:20:59,420
And to blow off a can of black powder, you'd kill somebody.

305
00:21:00,420 --> 00:21:06,320
For six months, construction boss James Harvey Strobridge safely directed his powder teams

306
00:21:06,321 --> 00:21:08,520
in Bloomer Cut.

307
00:21:08,521 --> 00:21:14,700
Then in July 1864, a worker accidentally struck a delayed charge with his hammer and drill.

308
00:21:14,701 --> 00:21:19,740
It hit the powder that was there and exploded.

309
00:21:19,741 --> 00:21:24,720
It killed a Portuguese, severely wounded a Frenchman, and blew out the right eye of James

310
00:21:24,721 --> 00:21:26,520
Harvey Strobridge.

311
00:21:26,521 --> 00:21:28,340
The next morning, he went to work.

312
00:21:30,480 --> 00:21:31,440
Tough guy.

313
00:21:33,200 --> 00:21:35,200
Yard by excruciating yard,

314
00:21:35,760 --> 00:21:37,080
the Central Pacific's workers

315
00:21:37,081 --> 00:21:38,960
pummeled their way through the mighty Sierra.

316
00:21:39,900 --> 00:21:43,300
By 1866, three years of effort

317
00:21:43,301 --> 00:21:45,400
had yielded only 40 miles of track.

318
00:21:47,080 --> 00:21:50,000
1,600 miles away, on the Great Plains,

319
00:21:50,520 --> 00:21:52,840
the Union Pacific was poised to surge ahead

320
00:21:52,841 --> 00:21:54,880
in the great race across the continent.

321
00:21:54,881 --> 00:21:59,880
powering their progress would be an innovative city on wheels.

322
00:22:02,880 --> 00:22:06,880
In 1868, to help fill the demand for labor on the transcontinental railroad,

323
00:22:06,881 --> 00:22:10,880
the United States signed the Burlingame Sewer Treaty with China.

324
00:22:10,881 --> 00:22:15,880
The agreement encouraged Chinese immigration to America.

325
00:22:15,881 --> 00:22:25,100
More than any other nation, Japan prides itself on the punctuality of its rail system.

326
00:22:26,880 --> 00:22:30,640
Some 7 million passengers board trains in Japan every day.

327
00:22:31,720 --> 00:22:34,960
The average lateness of arrival is a mere 6 seconds.

328
00:22:37,220 --> 00:22:44,540
In 1865, the two rival companies building the transcontinental railroad were decidedly behind schedule.

329
00:22:45,280 --> 00:22:49,320
In California, extreme terrain slowed one army of workers.

330
00:22:50,640 --> 00:22:53,520
In Nebraska, a lack of money stalled the other.

331
00:22:54,900 --> 00:23:01,060
As 1866 began, the Central Pacific had laid only 40 miles of track crossing the Sierra.

332
00:23:02,400 --> 00:23:08,320
1,600 miles to the east, the Union Pacific had advanced the same short distance across the Great Plains.

333
00:23:08,321 --> 00:23:15,040
But the Union Pacific gained momentum as it tapped a surging source of manpower.

334
00:23:16,160 --> 00:23:22,240
The Civil War had ended the previous year, and tens of thousands of unemployed veterans swelled its ranks.

335
00:23:24,540 --> 00:23:29,700
Union Pacific leader Thomas Durant decided now was the time to press west in earnest.

336
00:23:30,360 --> 00:23:35,920
To lead his army of ex-soldiers, he hired one of their own, General Grenville Dodge,

337
00:23:35,921 --> 00:23:39,460
an engineer who'd surveyed a rail route west before the war.

338
00:23:41,160 --> 00:23:45,900
Durant also hired former cavalry officer Jack Casement as his construction foreman.

339
00:23:47,040 --> 00:23:54,520
Jack Casement was barely over five foot tall, wore Cossack clothes, carried a bullwhip around,

340
00:23:55,000 --> 00:24:04,900
and ran the construction part of the railroad like an army, very highly disciplined,

341
00:24:04,901 --> 00:24:09,240
even though he was such a small man, nobody ever gave him any back talk.

342
00:24:10,560 --> 00:24:15,580
Casement and Dodge ensured efficiency by instilling their laborers with a sense of military precision.

343
00:24:16,900 --> 00:24:19,740
But they still faced a daunting logistical challenge.

344
00:24:20,920 --> 00:24:23,600
How could they house and feed this vast mobile workforce,

345
00:24:24,060 --> 00:24:27,000
as well as satisfy its voracious appetite for building materials?

346
00:24:28,740 --> 00:24:33,120
They found their answer with a system aptly called the City on Wheels.

347
00:24:33,121 --> 00:24:40,120
Locomotives pushed, not pulled, 20 or more cars, each tailor-made to support an army,

348
00:24:40,121 --> 00:24:44,000
building and living on the tracks.

349
00:24:44,001 --> 00:24:49,160
Several flat cars came loaded with tools and supplies.

350
00:24:49,161 --> 00:24:56,000
The rest of the cars were customized as blacksmith shops, dining coaches, kitchens and dormitories.

351
00:24:56,001 --> 00:25:01,460
Each rolling dormitory housed 50 men who slept in bunks stacked three high.

352
00:25:01,461 --> 00:25:08,180
The whole city on wheels concept of having your people just moving right along with the railhead

353
00:25:08,181 --> 00:25:13,700
was something that increased efficiency, allowed them to get those miles down.

354
00:25:14,320 --> 00:25:20,260
The Union Pacific's graders, track layers, and spike crews raced across the Nebraska plains.

355
00:25:21,020 --> 00:25:26,920
Laying as much as three miles of track a day, four men could put down four rails in 60 seconds.

356
00:25:26,921 --> 00:25:32,620
And you can imagine how it must have sounded with the shouting and the cursing probably.

357
00:25:33,480 --> 00:25:36,940
And get it down, get it spiked, get it spiked, Patty.

358
00:25:37,180 --> 00:25:39,540
Come on, don't, don't, don't loiter there.

359
00:25:39,620 --> 00:25:40,660
We don't have all day.

360
00:25:40,980 --> 00:25:44,220
Get her down all day long, 12 hours a day.

361
00:25:44,280 --> 00:25:48,360
It's almost impossible for us to believe in this day and age that they could have done that.

362
00:25:50,040 --> 00:25:53,600
As the Union Pacific tore further and further across the Great Plains,

363
00:25:53,960 --> 00:25:57,300
it penetrated the territories controlled by Native American tribes.

364
00:25:58,240 --> 00:25:59,320
Conflict was inevitable.

365
00:26:00,260 --> 00:26:03,700
They were going through the traditional hunting grounds

366
00:26:03,701 --> 00:26:06,940
of primarily the Cheyenne, the Sioux, the Arapaho.

367
00:26:08,100 --> 00:26:12,580
And those tribes were worried about the encroachment of the white man.

368
00:26:12,680 --> 00:26:15,260
They had seen what had happened further east.

369
00:26:15,460 --> 00:26:18,300
And then as the railroad came across,

370
00:26:18,440 --> 00:26:22,640
they saw them slaughtering the source of their food, their shelter.

371
00:26:22,641 --> 00:26:23,912
the buffalo.

372
00:26:23,913 --> 00:26:32,500
And so the presence of the railroads in that sense makes possible the destruction

373
00:26:32,501 --> 00:26:36,434
of the Native American way of life on the Great Plains.

374
00:26:36,435 --> 00:26:39,080
Some tribes submitted and made peace.

375
00:26:39,920 --> 00:26:45,140
Others lashed back at the iron horse. They started in small-scale attacks and

376
00:26:45,141 --> 00:26:50,780
larger attacks. The Union Pacific petitioned the federal government to

377
00:26:50,781 --> 00:26:56,960
send out US Army troops to protect them. Despite the threats from hostile tribes,

378
00:26:56,961 --> 00:27:04,040
the Union Pacific continued its streak across the plains. By the end of 1867,

379
00:27:04,041 --> 00:27:11,580
Union Pacific workers had laid 300 miles of track. At the same time, the Central

380
00:27:11,581 --> 00:27:14,730
Pacific was still slogging its way through the Sierra.

381
00:27:14,731 --> 00:27:16,480
They had laid only 80 miles of

382
00:27:16,481 --> 00:27:17,480
track.

383
00:27:17,481 --> 00:27:22,660
You have to look at the Central Pacific and how jealous they were of the Union Pacific

384
00:27:22,661 --> 00:27:27,520
because every 10 or 12 miles the Union Pacific could put down another town and they'd begin

385
00:27:27,521 --> 00:27:34,180
to sell out lots and they'd begin to reap all the benefits thereof.

386
00:27:34,181 --> 00:27:38,140
Despite the associates' lack of progress, they still had the confidence to engage in

387
00:27:38,141 --> 00:27:40,820
some arrogant posturing.

388
00:27:40,821 --> 00:27:46,180
Paulus Huntington was boasting all over Washington that they were going to get to Utah first

389
00:27:46,181 --> 00:27:50,320
and that they were going to reap all the coal fields of Utah and Wyoming.

390
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:53,800
The Union Pacific's owners were equally boastful.

391
00:27:55,020 --> 00:28:00,080
They claimed their crews could reach the west coast before the Central Pacific ever broke out of the Sierra.

392
00:28:02,340 --> 00:28:08,160
Grenville Dodge backed up his swagger by dispatching surveyors to search for a route bypassing the mountains.

393
00:28:08,161 --> 00:28:13,160
The workers of the Central Pacific defiantly pressed onward,

394
00:28:13,260 --> 00:28:15,760
but their success hinged on mastering

395
00:28:15,860 --> 00:28:18,200
the greatest engineering challenge either side

396
00:28:18,300 --> 00:28:22,300
would face, blasting a tunnel nearly half a mile long

397
00:28:22,400 --> 00:28:23,800
through a towering granite peak.

398
00:28:27,820 --> 00:28:30,520
After the Transcontinental Railroad was complete,

399
00:28:30,620 --> 00:28:33,520
buffalo herds crossing the track often delayed trains

400
00:28:33,620 --> 00:28:34,420
for hours.

401
00:28:38,160 --> 00:28:41,720
Interstate 80 from Sacramento to Donner Summit in the Sierra Nevada

402
00:28:41,721 --> 00:28:45,500
follows the same route pioneered by the Central Pacific Railroad.

403
00:28:46,740 --> 00:28:51,340
In winter, heavy snows sometimes force closure of the highway

404
00:28:51,341 --> 00:28:53,520
or slow traffic to a snail's pace.

405
00:28:54,600 --> 00:28:58,000
In these conditions, only vehicles equipped with chains or snow tires

406
00:28:58,001 --> 00:29:00,260
have any chance of making it over the summit.

407
00:29:02,700 --> 00:29:07,540
In the fall of 1866, the Central Pacific's workers rushed

408
00:29:07,541 --> 00:29:11,980
to lay as much track as possible before winter descended.

409
00:29:11,981 --> 00:29:14,540
During three years of backbreaking labor,

410
00:29:14,541 --> 00:29:17,740
they had advanced 90 miles, sculpting shelves

411
00:29:17,741 --> 00:29:21,460
along sheer mountain faces, bridging deep ravines

412
00:29:21,461 --> 00:29:24,100
with trestles, and blasting tunnels

413
00:29:24,101 --> 00:29:28,240
through some of the hardest rock in North America.

414
00:29:28,241 --> 00:29:31,980
Workers would have to build 18 tunnels through the Sierra.

415
00:29:31,981 --> 00:29:34,780
None of them posed a greater engineering challenge

416
00:29:34,781 --> 00:29:37,180
than the longest of seven needed at Donner Summit.

417
00:29:37,860 --> 00:29:42,320
It would measure 20 feet high and stretch more than 1,600 feet.

418
00:29:43,260 --> 00:29:47,640
The so-called summit tunnel was projected to take 15 months to finish.

419
00:29:48,340 --> 00:29:51,600
When they were building the tunnels up in the summit area,

420
00:29:52,120 --> 00:29:54,240
they were originally using black powder,

421
00:29:54,800 --> 00:29:58,860
and they were literally measuring progress in inches per day.

422
00:30:00,360 --> 00:30:02,940
Financial disaster loomed for the Central Pacific

423
00:30:02,941 --> 00:30:05,300
if the tunnels at the summit took too long to build.

424
00:30:06,940 --> 00:30:10,280
Its owners feared the delay would allow their Union Pacific rivals

425
00:30:10,281 --> 00:30:12,840
to streak all the way to the coast using an alternate route.

426
00:30:14,060 --> 00:30:16,420
Their only hope involved a dangerous gamble.

427
00:30:17,160 --> 00:30:20,440
Instead of black powder, they would use liquid nitroglycerin,

428
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:23,900
the era's most powerful and most unstable explosive.

429
00:30:25,320 --> 00:30:29,040
A Central Pacific chemist mixed the volatile compound on site.

430
00:30:29,041 --> 00:30:36,560
To prep the granite faces it would pulverize, workers hand-drilled holes 12 inches deep.

431
00:30:38,440 --> 00:30:43,840
A charge placed in a hole any shallower would direct too much of the blast away from the rock.

432
00:30:44,880 --> 00:30:47,760
Each drill team consisted of a driver and a shaker.

433
00:30:48,260 --> 00:30:54,940
And the shaker would hold the drill while the driver would swing a sledgehammer and strike the end.

434
00:30:54,941 --> 00:30:59,640
each time he would strike the end, the shaker would give a little twist,

435
00:31:00,120 --> 00:31:02,960
and the driver would drive again and give a little twist,

436
00:31:03,060 --> 00:31:04,160
and that's why he's called a shaker.

437
00:31:04,480 --> 00:31:08,660
He's twisting the drill around to even out

438
00:31:08,661 --> 00:31:11,380
so that he can drill an even hole all the way in.

439
00:31:13,040 --> 00:31:15,000
Nitro doubled the pace of progress.

440
00:31:16,140 --> 00:31:19,300
Blast teams attacked both ends of the summit tunnel simultaneously.

441
00:31:20,200 --> 00:31:24,580
To further speed productivity, engineers devised a radical strategy.

442
00:31:24,940 --> 00:31:36,608
In order to speed the work on the summit tunnel, they actually drilled a shaft down at the center where the tunnel was to be, and then started going outward from that

443
00:31:36,609 --> 00:31:37,309
center.

444
00:31:37,680 --> 00:31:40,940
So they actually had four faces that they were cutting in.

445
00:31:41,900 --> 00:31:48,680
Eventually it got down to where they couldn't throw the rock out, and they began to use a rope hoist with a bucket and get the rock out of the shaft.

446
00:31:48,681 --> 00:31:51,480
And then they brought this old locomotive up from Sacramento.

447
00:31:51,481 --> 00:31:57,580
It was all stripped down and they hooked it up over the top of the shaft and they made a winch that was connected to the engine

448
00:31:57,581 --> 00:32:02,540
So they were able to bring the heavy burden of all this debris that was blasted up out of the shaft

449
00:32:04,360 --> 00:32:06,600
Work on the tunnel still progressed slowly

450
00:32:08,640 --> 00:32:13,820
Then fall turned to winter and the frigid skies dumped record snows on the Sierra Nevada

451
00:32:15,160 --> 00:32:19,880
44 separate snowstorms stalled incoming trains bearing food and supplies

452
00:32:19,881 --> 00:32:25,040
The railroad's laborers suffered from pneumonia, frostbite, and malnutrition.

453
00:32:25,041 --> 00:32:32,780
Avalanches killed many. Dutifully, crews continued their work.

454
00:32:32,781 --> 00:32:40,880
As the snow piled deeper, they actually dug snow tunnels to go from their camp to the tunnel face,

455
00:32:40,881 --> 00:32:43,850
so they never had to come out into the weather.

456
00:32:43,851 --> 00:32:46,820
They could go directly from their cabins out to

457
00:32:46,821 --> 00:32:51,920
their work site on the tunnels and then go back again.

458
00:32:51,921 --> 00:32:54,520
Clearing the snow drifts from the tracks already laid

459
00:32:54,521 --> 00:32:56,760
was imperative to maintaining the flow of supplies

460
00:32:56,761 --> 00:32:58,920
from Sacramento.

461
00:32:58,921 --> 00:33:02,120
One of the things that they did to deal with the snow

462
00:33:02,121 --> 00:33:05,660
was that they designed a big, bucker snow plow.

463
00:33:05,661 --> 00:33:08,500
This is a railroad car with wheels of its own

464
00:33:08,501 --> 00:33:11,200
and a big wedge on the front and an enclosed body.

465
00:33:11,201 --> 00:33:14,360
And it would be pushed by seven or eight locomotives

466
00:33:14,361 --> 00:33:15,760
to clear the snow off.

467
00:33:16,760 --> 00:33:19,700
The gargantuan snowplow was 11 feet high,

468
00:33:19,960 --> 00:33:22,180
10 feet wide, and 30 feet long.

469
00:33:23,020 --> 00:33:26,500
But despite its size, it was only moderately successful.

470
00:33:27,580 --> 00:33:30,100
Deep snow drifts often stopped it in its tracks.

471
00:33:30,840 --> 00:33:34,640
The only option then was to back up, get a running start,

472
00:33:35,080 --> 00:33:37,140
and hammer the snow like a battering ram.

473
00:33:37,980 --> 00:33:40,440
The impact sometimes derailed the machine,

474
00:33:40,980 --> 00:33:44,020
which plowed instead into crewmen working near the tracks.

475
00:33:44,360 --> 00:33:45,520
killing them instantly.

476
00:33:46,980 --> 00:33:50,200
Another countermeasure against the harsh winter worked more effectively.

477
00:33:52,160 --> 00:33:55,800
Crews constructed 37 miles of wooden snowsheds around the tracks,

478
00:33:56,440 --> 00:33:58,920
ensuring that their rail-building supplies from Sacramento

479
00:33:58,921 --> 00:34:01,240
could make their way up the frozen Sierra.

480
00:34:03,720 --> 00:34:07,560
The sheds were crafted from 65 million linear feet of timber,

481
00:34:08,260 --> 00:34:11,300
held together by 9 million tons of bolts and spikes.

482
00:34:11,301 --> 00:34:21,920
spring finally melted the crippling blanket of snow on August 29 1867 13 months after the drillers

483
00:34:21,921 --> 00:34:29,620
and blasters began their work they completed the summit tunnel two months ahead of schedule the

484
00:34:29,621 --> 00:34:34,280
way was now clear to lay a continuous line of track down the eastern slope of the mountains

485
00:34:34,281 --> 00:34:35,600
and into Nevada.

486
00:34:37,180 --> 00:34:39,820
Finally, the Central Pacific was picking up steam.

487
00:34:41,040 --> 00:34:44,680
At the same time, the Union Pacific was slowing to a crawl,

488
00:34:45,260 --> 00:34:47,240
smacking headlong into the Rocky Mountains.

489
00:34:48,120 --> 00:34:50,600
In late 1867, early 1868,

490
00:34:51,420 --> 00:34:54,340
as the Union Pacific was getting into western Wyoming

491
00:34:54,341 --> 00:34:57,780
and beginning to contend with its first engineering challenges,

492
00:34:57,960 --> 00:35:00,280
with tunnels, with a lot of rock cutting and blasting

493
00:35:00,281 --> 00:35:03,060
through the Wasatch Mountains and the canyons

494
00:35:03,061 --> 00:35:04,580
down to the Salt Lake Valley.

495
00:35:04,960 --> 00:35:07,960
This is where the Mormon colony in Salt Lake City,

496
00:35:08,140 --> 00:35:11,980
the Latter-day Saints, were able to step in and help out.

497
00:35:13,040 --> 00:35:16,120
20 years earlier, Brigham Young and his Mormon followers

498
00:35:16,121 --> 00:35:19,700
had settled in the Utah desert to escape religious persecution.

499
00:35:22,040 --> 00:35:25,480
Young negotiated a $2 million contract with the Union Pacific,

500
00:35:26,260 --> 00:35:29,520
providing 4,000 Mormon workers to grade and tunnel

501
00:35:29,521 --> 00:35:32,420
through 120 miles of mountainous terrain.

502
00:35:33,060 --> 00:35:41,060
The Union Pacific's track layers, hundreds of miles behind the Mormon workers, were toiling their way slowly across Wyoming.

503
00:35:41,061 --> 00:35:46,060
But the highest mountains were behind them, and their pace was soon to quicken.

504
00:35:46,061 --> 00:35:54,060
500 miles west, Central Pacific workers had finally emerged from the Sierra and were laying track across Nevada's desert.

505
00:35:54,061 --> 00:36:03,180
As 1869 began, the two rival railroad builders were racing toward each other in a full-on sprint.

506
00:36:05,880 --> 00:36:11,140
Whichever company moved faster would reap the greatest gold mine in land and government subsidies.

507
00:36:13,220 --> 00:36:15,860
America breathlessly awaited the outcome.

508
00:36:15,861 --> 00:36:25,860
Wyoming's 650-foot Dale Creek trestle built by the Union Pacific was the longest on the

509
00:36:25,861 --> 00:36:27,674
Transcontinental Railroad.

510
00:36:27,675 --> 00:36:32,000
The 150-foot-high wooden structure swayed dangerously, even in

511
00:36:32,001 --> 00:36:38,776
moderate winds, requiring trains to slow to 4 miles per hour.

512
00:36:38,777 --> 00:36:42,220
This obscure patch of desert in

513
00:36:42,221 --> 00:36:51,220
The road in northern Utah lies 690 miles east of Sacramento and 1,087 miles west of Omaha.

514
00:36:51,221 --> 00:36:57,220
Little about Promontory Summit impresses the eye, but a century and a half ago, fate decreed

515
00:36:57,221 --> 00:37:03,220
that here America would finally be linked by a spine of iron and truly become one nation

516
00:37:03,221 --> 00:37:08,220
indivisible. In early 1869, the race to complete the

517
00:37:08,221 --> 00:37:11,020
to complete the transcontinental railroad was nearing its finish.

518
00:37:12,580 --> 00:37:15,380
The Central Pacific tore east across Nevada,

519
00:37:16,100 --> 00:37:18,600
the Union Pacific west across Utah.

520
00:37:19,560 --> 00:37:22,320
The rival companies had yet to determine their meeting point,

521
00:37:22,860 --> 00:37:27,220
and each mile gained by one meant land and money lost by the other.

522
00:37:28,740 --> 00:37:32,240
To prepare the way, each company sent out grading crews

523
00:37:32,241 --> 00:37:34,880
as far as 150 miles ahead of the track layers.

524
00:37:35,840 --> 00:37:38,820
North of the Great Salt Lake, they finally met,

525
00:37:39,360 --> 00:37:41,240
but the rival crews passed one another,

526
00:37:41,880 --> 00:37:45,180
carving parallel roadbeds as close as 100 feet apart.

527
00:37:45,840 --> 00:37:49,760
In this area, the Central Pacific was the first one that arrived,

528
00:37:49,920 --> 00:37:54,900
so they got to use the best grade to maintain that 2% grade.

529
00:37:55,460 --> 00:37:58,260
The Union Pacific, when they got here a couple of weeks later,

530
00:37:58,820 --> 00:38:02,040
they had to take basically second-best grade.

531
00:38:02,041 --> 00:38:04,760
The Central Pacific cut through the blue limestone,

532
00:38:04,880 --> 00:38:08,960
and just downhill from it, the Union Pacific cut through the limestone.

533
00:38:09,400 --> 00:38:12,000
You can see that they're just a few dozen yards apart.

534
00:38:12,840 --> 00:38:17,420
In April, government leaders finally pressured the companies to agree on a meeting point.

535
00:38:18,500 --> 00:38:23,280
In Washington, executives hammered out a compromise and chose Promontory Summit.

536
00:38:23,840 --> 00:38:31,560
As the tracks drew closer together and it became apparent that the Pacific Railroad was going to be a reality and not just a dream,

537
00:38:31,960 --> 00:38:35,500
They began feeling that they were truly part of a great national work.

538
00:38:35,940 --> 00:38:40,540
They began to know that they were doing something that would outlive them, outlast them,

539
00:38:41,040 --> 00:38:44,960
that even down to the most anonymous man with a shovel in his hands,

540
00:38:45,320 --> 00:38:48,200
this was going to be a work for the ages, and they had a part in it.

541
00:38:48,980 --> 00:38:53,640
A nation still reeling from the Civil War and the assassination of Abraham Lincoln

542
00:38:53,641 --> 00:38:55,480
now had a reason to celebrate.

543
00:38:56,060 --> 00:39:01,260
Some historians have described it as it relates to a century later sending a man to the moon.

544
00:39:01,560 --> 00:39:05,440
You're trying something that a lot of people say can't be done.

545
00:39:05,441 --> 00:39:10,200
You're doing it much faster than anybody thinks it can be done.

546
00:39:10,201 --> 00:39:14,680
And you're doing it at a time when the country really needs a lift.

547
00:39:14,681 --> 00:39:18,440
As Americans anticipated the railroad's completion,

548
00:39:18,441 --> 00:39:21,280
Central Pacific construction chief Charlie Crocker

549
00:39:21,281 --> 00:39:25,120
and Union Pacific leader Thomas Durant had a score to settle.

550
00:39:25,121 --> 00:39:28,920
Their race was over, but not their rivalry.

551
00:39:28,921 --> 00:39:33,820
Each bragged that his crews could lay more track in a single day than the others.

552
00:39:34,820 --> 00:39:39,140
And it got to the point Charles Crocker with the Central Pacific said,

553
00:39:39,141 --> 00:39:44,880
hey, my guys can lay 10 miles of track in a day.

554
00:39:46,080 --> 00:39:49,880
And Durant said, you know, no way.

555
00:39:50,000 --> 00:39:52,640
They can't beat our record of 7.5 miles.

556
00:39:53,100 --> 00:39:56,360
They wound up making a bet of about $10,000

557
00:39:56,361 --> 00:40:00,720
that the Central Pacific could lay 10 miles of track in a day.

558
00:40:01,680 --> 00:40:04,480
At dawn on April 29, 1869,

559
00:40:05,040 --> 00:40:07,120
Crocker's best crewmen bolted into action.

560
00:40:07,780 --> 00:40:09,020
With machine-like precision,

561
00:40:09,560 --> 00:40:12,720
they progressed at an average speed of one mile of track per hour.

562
00:40:13,420 --> 00:40:15,940
By 7 p.m., 12 hours after they started,

563
00:40:16,820 --> 00:40:19,540
they'd laid 3,520 rails

564
00:40:19,541 --> 00:40:24,260
using 55,000 spikes and 14,080 bolts.

565
00:40:24,261 --> 00:40:31,140
They wound up in one day laying just a little over 10 miles of track,

566
00:40:31,340 --> 00:40:35,340
which is still the record for hand-laid track anywhere in the world.

567
00:40:36,040 --> 00:40:38,500
And so Durant lost his bet.

568
00:40:39,360 --> 00:40:46,560
And as near as we know, he never paid Crocker the $10,000 that he owed him.

569
00:40:47,800 --> 00:40:49,600
One more drama remained.

570
00:40:50,160 --> 00:40:52,420
The moment the two rail lines were joined,

571
00:40:53,160 --> 00:40:57,320
A special ceremony was planned in which trains from each company would meet at the spot.

572
00:40:59,840 --> 00:41:04,020
Central Pacific President Leland Stanford set out for Utah from Sacramento,

573
00:41:04,560 --> 00:41:07,420
while the Union Pacific's Thomas Durant traveled from Omaha.

574
00:41:11,240 --> 00:41:15,360
May 10, 1869 was a beautiful, bright, sunny morning.

575
00:41:15,940 --> 00:41:19,700
The Union Pacific was coming from the east.

576
00:41:19,701 --> 00:41:28,160
The locomotive, the number 119, was pulling Thomas Durant and his entourage right here

577
00:41:28,161 --> 00:41:30,020
to the last spike site.

578
00:41:30,021 --> 00:41:36,000
And Stanford and his party were coming from the west, being pulled by the Jupiter.

579
00:41:37,180 --> 00:41:38,720
And they met here.

580
00:41:41,760 --> 00:41:46,780
The ceremony called for Stanford and Durant to drive not one, but four spikes.

581
00:41:46,781 --> 00:41:52,280
two gold, one silver, and a fourth made of gold, silver, and iron.

582
00:41:53,300 --> 00:41:56,220
A telegraph operator stood by to signal the news.

583
00:42:07,900 --> 00:42:12,020
History tells us that the telegrapher who sat about right here,

584
00:42:12,220 --> 00:42:15,760
his wire was connected to the telegraph line right up here,

585
00:42:15,761 --> 00:42:20,440
and the maul was wired as well as the last spike.

586
00:42:20,441 --> 00:42:25,440
So presumably, each time that the sledge hit the spike,

587
00:42:25,441 --> 00:42:29,280
it would send a pulse, which would be a signal

588
00:42:29,281 --> 00:42:31,940
that the last spike was driven.

589
00:42:31,941 --> 00:42:33,780
When the rails were joined,

590
00:42:33,781 --> 00:42:35,980
the telegraph operator wired a message

591
00:42:35,981 --> 00:42:38,520
as understated as it was accurate.

592
00:42:38,521 --> 00:42:42,880
He tapped out D-O-N-E.

593
00:42:45,760 --> 00:42:50,140
And that signal, telegraphed all the way across the country,

594
00:42:50,640 --> 00:42:56,300
was the signal for bells to ring, cannons to be fired in communities,

595
00:42:56,820 --> 00:43:01,160
celebrations all the way across the country, all happening at the same time,

596
00:43:01,520 --> 00:43:05,460
and linked by telegraph to this instantaneous event.

597
00:43:07,080 --> 00:43:09,780
Six years after the first track had been laid,

598
00:43:09,781 --> 00:43:13,360
the dream of a nation united by rail was realized.

599
00:43:13,361 --> 00:43:17,360
The Transcontinental Railroad connected the two halves of the country.

600
00:43:17,361 --> 00:43:20,360
Without the railroad, it would have been two separate countries.

601
00:43:20,361 --> 00:43:23,360
Before the Transcontinental Railroad was built,

602
00:43:23,361 --> 00:43:30,360
it literally took four months to go from Omaha to the west coast by wagon.

603
00:43:30,361 --> 00:43:34,360
And once the train was built, you could get a first-class ticket

604
00:43:34,361 --> 00:43:38,360
and be in San Francisco from Omaha in four to five days.

605
00:43:38,361 --> 00:43:42,360
The most amazing thing about the Transcontinental Railroad

606
00:43:42,361 --> 00:43:44,200
Railroad is the people.

607
00:43:44,300 --> 00:43:48,760
The people that envisioned it, the people that built it,

608
00:43:48,860 --> 00:43:50,560
the people that financed it.

609
00:43:50,660 --> 00:43:53,500
The people that did this are phenomenal.

610
00:43:53,600 --> 00:43:56,500
It's a work of giants, truly a work of giants.

